  <!-- Adapter vs Converter -->
  <h2 id="adapter-vs-converter">Adapter vs Converter: What's the Difference?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Adapter:</strong> Changes the plug shape so your device fits foreign outlets. Does NOT change voltage.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Converter/Transformer:</strong> Changes electrical voltage (e.g., 220V to 110V). Required for
    single-voltage devices.
  </p>

  <h3>Do You Need a Converter?</h3>
  <p>
    Check your device's power brick. If it says <strong>"INPUT: 100-240V"</strong>, it's dual-voltage and only
    needs an adapter. This includes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>All laptop chargers</li>
    <li>All phone/tablet chargers</li>
    <li>Most camera chargers</li>
    <li>Electric shavers (usually)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You need a converter for single-voltage devices like:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Hair dryers (usually)</li>
    <li>Curling irons (usually)</li>
    <li>Some older electronics</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Tip:</strong> For hair tools, consider buying dual-voltage versions or getting them locally.
    Converters are heavy and can damage devices if used incorrectly.
  </p>

  <!-- Plug Types by Region -->
