  <h3>Americas</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>USA, Canada, Mexico:</strong> Type A/B (flat pins)</li>
    <li><strong>Brazil:</strong> Type N (unique to Brazil)</li>
    <li><strong>Argentina:</strong> Type C/I</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Europe</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Most of Europe:</strong> Type C/F (two round pins)</li>
    <li><strong>UK, Ireland:</strong> Type G (three rectangular)</li>
    <li><strong>Switzerland:</strong> Type J</li>
    <li><strong>Italy:</strong> Type L</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Asia & Middle East</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>China:</strong> Type A/C/I</li>
    <li><strong>Japan:</strong> Type A/B (same as US)</li>
    <li><strong>India:</strong> Type C/D/M</li>
    <li><strong>Thailand:</strong> Type A/B/C</li>
    <li><strong>UAE, Saudi Arabia:</strong> Type G (UK style)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Oceania & Africa</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Australia, New Zealand:</strong> Type I</li>
    <li><strong>South Africa:</strong> Type M/N</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Safety -->
  <h2 id="safety">Safety Certifications to Look For</h2>
  <p>
    Not all travel adapters are created equal. Look for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>CE:</strong> European conformity standard</li>
    <li><strong>FCC:</strong> US Federal Communications Commission</li>
    <li><strong>UL:</strong> Underwriters Laboratories (rigorous testing)</li>
    <li><strong>RoHS:</strong> Restriction of hazardous substances</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Warning signs:</strong> No certifications listed, ultra-cheap pricing, no brand name, no safety
    fuse. A bad adapter can damage expensive devices or cause fire hazards.
  </p>

